{"text": "{\"query\": \"\u7a32\u5ddd\u4f1a organized crime Japan yakuza\", \"results\": [{\"title\": \"Yakuza - Wikipedia\", \"url\": \"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Yakuza\", \"content\": \"Yakuza (Japanese: \u30e4\u30af\u30b6; IPA: ( \\\"Help:IPA/Japanese\\\"); English: /j\u0259\u02c8ku\u02d0z\u0259, \u02c8j\u00e6ku\u02d0z\u0259/), also known as gokud\u014d (\u6975\u9053; \\\"the extreme path\\\", IPA: ( \\\"Help:IPA/Japanese\\\")), or simply the Japanese mafia, are members of transnational organized crime syndicates originating in Japan. The Japanese police and media (by request of the police) call them b\u014dryokudan (\u66b4\u529b\u56e3; \\\"violent groups\\\", IPA: ( \\\"Help:IPA/Japanese\\\")), while the yakuza call themselves ninky\u014d dantai (\u4efb\u4fa0\u56e3\u4f53; \\\"chivalrous organizations\\\", IPA: ( \\\"Help:IPA/Japanese\\\")). The English equivalent for the term yakuza is gangster, meaning an individual involved in a Mafia-like criminal organization, but is generally used in English-speaking countries to refer to the criminal organization as a whole. [...] ### Designated yakuza (Shitei B\u014dryokudan)\\n\\n[edit\\\")]\\n\\nA designated yakuza (\u6307\u5b9a\u66b4\u529b\u56e3, Shitei B\u014dryokudan) is a \\\"particularly harmful\\\" yakuza group registered by the Prefectural Public Safety Commissions \\\"National Public Safety Commission (Japan)\\\") under the Act on Prevention of Unjust Acts by Organized Crime Group Members (\u66b4\u529b\u56e3\u5bfe\u7b56\u6cd5, B\u014dryokudan Taisaku H\u014d) enacted in 1991. Groups are designated as Shitei B\u014dryokudan (designated yakuza) if their members take advantage of the gang's influence to do business, are structured to have one leader, and have a large portion of their members hold criminal records. After the Act on Prevention of Unjust Acts by Organized Crime Group Members was enacted, many yakuza syndicates made efforts to restructure to appear more professional and legitimate. [...] Tools\\n\\n