onship of the yakuza with the government is. > How the State has Benefited Yakuza. yakuza is such a case. Also during this time, the yakuza was an. would be adjusted five times to better target the yakuza (NPA, 2015). several approaches through legislation and law enforcement, yakuza activity to. Although yakuza are Japanese -based crime groups, they can lead to. as an organized crime group, the yakuza have been actively deterred by foreign. Japanese government in certain regards in combatting the yakuza. The activity and functions of yakuza groups have also adapted and been. part icipants in the yakuza. In general, yakuza organizations have moved away from gambling to other. government and its relationship to the yakuza has been multi -layered --- the state. unfriendliness toward the criminal group and an \u201canti -Yakuza movement\u201d (Reilly,. magazines\\\" whic h portray yakuza activity and culture (Reilly, 2014, p. organized crime, yakuza have not necessarily gone under the public radar. The yakuza, like many organized crime groups, are not simply ad hoc,. Yakuza: The warlords of Japanese organized crime. Chapter 11: The Japanese yakuza.\", \"score\": 0.75290436}, {\"title\": \"Yakuza Organized Crime in Japan: Origins and Decline - LegalClarity\", \"url\": \"https://legalclarity.org/yakuza-organized-crime-in-japan-origins-and-decline\", \"content\": \"Title: Yakuza Organized Crime in Japan: Origins and Decline - LegalClarity\\n# Yakuza Organized Crime in Japan: Origins and Decline. Japan's yakuza trace their roots back centuries, but strict laws and social exclusion have pushed these once-powerful syndicates into steep decline. Japan\u2019s yakuza represent one of the world\u2019s oldest and most structured organized crime networks, with roots stretching back to the 1600s and an estimated combined revenue that may reach into the trillions of yen annually. Unlike criminal syndicates in most countries, these groups have historically operated in plain sight, maintaining storefronts and offices with name plates on the door. That openness has made them an unusual legal target: Japan\u2019s approach combines criminal law, civil liability for syndicate bosses, and a sweeping system of social exclusion that reaches into the everyday lives of members and former members alike. By the end of 2024, total membership had fallen to roughly 18,800, less than a fifth of its 1960s peak, but the organizations remain deeply embedded in Japanese society and increasingly active overseas. The yakuza trace their lineage to two groups of outcasts who emerged during the Tokugawa Shogunate (1603\u20131868). Many tekiya came from the burakumin social class, a caste of outcasts who sat below Japan\u2019s four-tiered feudal hierarchy. These appointed leaders were called oyabun, or \u201cfoster parent,\u201d a title that still sits at the top of every yakuza hierarchy. Bakuto were known for covering themselves in elaborate tattoos, a tradition that carried forward into modern yakuza culture. Even now, specific syndicates may identify themselves as descended from tekiya or bakuto depending on how they earn the majority of their income, and both lineages contributed rituals that still define yakuza initiation and discipline. The ritual is not symbolic in the casual sense; it creates an obligation that members treat as binding for life. The foundation is a national criminal statute; on top of that sit amendments creating civil liability for bosses, enhanced designations for violent groups, and local ordinances that squeeze members out of everyday life. Act on Prevention of Unjust Acts by Organized Crime Group Members The law gives the National Public Safety Commission authority to formally designate specific organizations as boryokudan, or designated crime groups.\", \"score\": 0.752468}, {\"title\": \"A Necessary Evil? Negotiating the Future of Yakuza Control and Organized Crime Legislation in Japan | FSI\", \"url\": \"https://cddrl.fsi.stanford.edu/lad/publication/necessary-evil-negotiating-future-yakuza-control-and-organized-crime-legislation-japan\", \"content\": \"The yakuza is an organized and transnational criminal syndicate system based in Japan with a monopoly over the nation's criminal underworld. Honor-bound to a code similar to that of the Japanese samurai, the yakuza have deep historical roots and widespread influence in Japanese society. Their existence is widely known, highly visible, and largely accepted by ordinary Japanese citizens and law enforcement agencies, who take little issue with them as long as they do not threaten public affairs or safety. Since the yakuza regulate-and essentially maintain-the criminal underworld through a tightly controlled membership network, and because the public is rarely ever impacted by their activities (i.e., they do not fear random violence or attacks), the yakuza's continued existence is commonly [...] or attacks), the yakuza's continued existence is commonly justified by a \\\"necessary evil\\\" argument. Without them, the criminal underworld in Japan would be highly unregulated, unpredictable, and susceptible to undesirable foreign infiltration or domination. Despite the presence of the yakuza, or perhaps because of them, Japan enjoys a \\\"low-crime\\\" status relative to other countries (Leonardsen 2004). [...] All LAD Publications\", \"score\": 0.7067782}, {\"title\": \"Yakuza | History, Meaning, Rituals, & Facts | Britannica\", \"url\": \"https://www.britannica.com/topic/yakuza\", \"content\": \"The yakuza are Japanese organized crime groups, or b\u014dryokudan, with a structure similar to the Italian Mafia. The leader is the oyabun (\\\"boss,\\\" or \\\"parent status\\\"), and followers are kobun (\\\"prot\u00e9g\u00e9s,\\\" or \\\"child status\\\"). The yakuza maintain a rigid hierarchy, discipline, and a right-wing ultranationalistic ideology. Traditionally, kobun took a blood oath of allegiance. Though declining, members who break the code historically show penance by cutting off their little finger and presenting it to the oyabun. Gang membership reached approximately 184,000 in the early 1960s but declined to around 80,000 by the early 21st century.\\n\\n  1 Britannica Source\\n\\n  1. yakuza [...] Top Questions\\n\\n What is the yakuza?\\n\\n  The yakuza are Japanese organized crime groups, formally called b\u014dryokudan (\\\"violence groups\\\"). The term yakuza can refer to individual gangsters, criminal organizations, or Japanese organized crime in general.\\n\\n  Yakuza have rituals similar to those of samurai and often have elaborate tattoos. They engage in many rackets and control various businesses in major Japanese cities. They are also involved in criminal activities worldwide.\\n\\n  The word yakuza (\\\"good for nothing\\\") comes from a losing hand in a Japanese card game. The origin of the yakuza is uncertain, but they may have come from gangs of masterless samurai or from groups of bandits and gamblers in Japan\u2019s feudal period.\\n\\n  2 Britannica Sources\\n\\n  1. yakuza\\n  2. Definition of yakuza [...] yakuza, Japanese gangsters, members of what are formally called b\u014dryokudan (\u201cviolence groups\u201d), or Mafia-like criminal organizations. In Japan and elsewhere, especially in the West, the term yakuza can be used to refer to individual gangsters or criminals as well as to their organized groups and to Japanese organized crime in general. Yakuza adopt samurai-like rituals and often bear elaborate body tattoos. They engage in extortion, blackmail, smuggling, prostitution, drug trafficking, gambling, loan sharking, day-labour contracting, and other rackets and control many restaurants, bars, trucking companies, talent agencies, taxi fleets, factories, and other businesses in major Japanese cities. They are also involved in criminal activities worldwide.\", \"score\": 0.67885995}, {\"title\": \"Yakuza | Law | Research Starters | EBSCOhost\", \"url\": \"https://www.ebsco.com/research-starters/law/yakuza\", \"content\": \"\\\" name=\\\"description\\\">\\\" property=\\\"og:description\\\">\\\" name=\\\"twitter:description\\\">\\n\\nRESEARCH STARTER\\n\\n# Yakuza\\n\\nYakuza refers to organized criminal gangs in Japan, comparable to the American mafia, involved in various illegal activities such as gambling, prostitution, and connections to political and financial institutions. With over 102,000 members globally, Yakuza groups are known for their distinct culture, including elaborate tattoos and strict codes of conduct. The roots of Yakuza can be traced back to the early 17th century, with origins in eccentric samurai known as kabuki-mono, and later evolved into groups of gamblers and street vendors. [...] Publication type: Periodical\\n\\n## Full article\\n\\nYakuzais the name given to organized criminal gangs in Japan. They are a collection of separate gangs or clans comparable to the American mafia and using similar operations to prove their power. Yakuza members are considered to be criminals whose violence and existence has affected many aspects of Japanese life, from illegal gambling and prostitution rackets to high-level political and financial organizations. They numbered about 18,800 members in 2024. [...] Greedy.; National Treasure.\\n\\n4 of 4\\n\\n## Full article\\n\\nYakuzais the name given to organized criminal gangs in Japan. They are a collection of separate gangs or clans comparable to the American mafia and using similar operations to prove their power. Yakuza members are considered to be criminals whose violence and existence has affected many aspects of Japanese life, from illegal gambling and prostitution rackets to high-level political and financial organizations. They numbered about 18,800 members in 2024.\", \"score\": 0.66569364}, {\"title\": \"B\u014dryokudan | Yakuza, Syndicates & Gangs | Britannica\", \"url\": \"https://www.britannica.com/topic/boryokudan\", \"content\": \"Title: B\u014dryokudan | Yakuza, Syndicates & Gangs | Britannica\\n**b\u014dryokudan**, any of various Japanese criminal gangs, many of which combined in the 20th century into Mafia-like organizations. The word was embraced by Japanese officials in the late 20th century to serve as a replacement for the term *yakuza* (\u201cgood for nothing\u201d), which had taken on increasingly positive connotations in Japan. Members of *b\u014dryokudan*, themselves often called *yakuza*, or *gyangu* (\u201cgangster\u201d), adopt samurai-like rituals and often bear elaborate body tattoos. *B\u014dryokudan* have also been involved in criminal activities outside Japan. The relationship between gangs and police in Japan is a complicated one; *yakuza*-owned businesses and gang headquarters are often clearly marked, and gang whereabouts and activities are often known to Japanese police without the latter\u2019s taking any action. While their methods are often questionable, they have been known to perform charitable acts, such as donating and delivering supplies to earthquake victims. It is in part because of this dual nature as criminals and humanitarians and because of the idolization of *yakuza* in global popular media that the national police agency in Japan in the 1990s instated the name *b\u014dryokudan* in an antigang law to reinforce the criminal nature of *yakuza* organizations, which had endeared themselves to the public as societal \u201cunderdogs\u201d and self-proclaimed *ninky\u014d dantai* (\u201cchivalrous organizations\u201d). Despite police crackdowns, at the beginning of the 21st century there were some 80,000 gang members in Japan, organized into hundreds of gangs and several prominent gang conglomerates. The origin of the yakuza is uncertain, but they may have come from gangs of masterless samurai or from groups of bandits and gamblers in Japan\u2019s feudal period. Despite their criminal behavior, the yakuza style themselves as ninky\u014d dantai (chivalrous organizations) and have been known to perform charitable acts. The yakuza are Japanese organized crime groups, or *b\u014dryokudan*, with a structure similar to the Italian Mafia. **yakuza**, Japanese gangsters, members of what are formally called *b\u014dryokudan* (\u201cviolence groups\u201d), or Mafia-like criminal organizations. It is in part because of the dual nature of their relationship with police\u2014as both criminals and sometimes humanitarians\u2014and the idolization of criminal groups as \u201cunderdogs\u201d in popular media that the Japanese police agency in the 1990s instated the name *b\u014dryokudan* in an antigang law to reinforce the criminal nature of yakuza organizations.\", \"score\": 0.64826655}, {\"title\": \"The Yakuza: Unraveling the World of Japan\u2019s Organized Crime\", \"url\": \"https://www.shimizuart.org/post/the-yakuza-unraveling-the-world-of-japan-s-organized-crime\", \"content\": \"# The Yakuza: Unraveling the World of Japan\u2019s Organized Crime. The Yakuza, Japan's infamous organized crime syndicates, are as enigmatic as they are controversial. Known for their strict codes of conduct, intricate rituals, and influence in both legal and illegal enterprises, the Yakuza have become a significant, if shadowy, aspect of Japanese culture. This blog delves into the history, structure, activities, and societal impact of the Yakuza, painting a comprehensive picture of this complex organization. ### **The Origins of the Yakuza**. The Yakuza operate as highly organized groups with a pyramid-like structure resembling a feudal system:. ### **Yakuza Rituals and Symbolism**. The Yakuza are known for their elaborate rituals and distinctive symbols, which reinforce loyalty and identity. Yakuza members are famously adorned with intricate, full-body tattoos. While the Yakuza are notorious for illegal activities, their operations often straddle the line between crime and legitimate business. * **Drug Trafficking**: Despite public claims of avoiding narcotics, many Yakuza groups are deeply involved in drug distribution. Yakuza groups often invest in legitimate businesses, including construction, real estate, and finance. Historically, the Yakuza have maintained connections with politicians and law enforcement, leveraging these relationships to influence policy and avoid prosecution. ### **The Yakuza\u2019s Role in Society**. In disasters like the **Kobe Earthquake (1995)**\u00a0and the **Fukushima Tsunami (2011)**, Yakuza groups were among the first to provide aid, distributing supplies and offering shelter. In recent decades, the Yakuza's influence has waned due to government crackdowns and shifting societal attitudes. #### **Anti-Yakuza Legislation**. Laws like the **Boryokudan Countermeasure Law (1992)**\u00a0have criminalized Yakuza activities, making it harder for them to operate openly. Businesses and individuals are also penalized for associating with known Yakuza groups. ### **The Future of the Yakuza**. As Japan continues to modernize, the Yakuza\u2019s role as a cultural and criminal force may fade, leaving behind a legacy marked by both infamy and intrigue. The Yakuza are an indelible part of Japan\u2019s social fabric, embodying a unique blend of tradition, crime, and cultural identity. While their influence may be waning, the mystique surrounding the Yakuza ensures their place in the annals of Japanese history and popular culture.\", \"score\": 0.6437107}]}"}